ruleResultT -- what if and while read when a rule is called from an action body: a match with a value, a match with an
empty capture, and a failure; and reading a piece by its label afterwards. Both grammars, pinned as read (Clay's dispatch,
2026-11-19; the ruling is Tony's, from these rows).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's stop(), then on the OLD road (incant/grammar) a call of the row's action; on the NEW road (kantGrammar) rrNew on
it, then the call. A sentinel on stderr ends each. Each row reads `stdout | R<REFUSED lines> x<exit>` and the sentinel.

The rule rv is [ then an OPTIONAL val then ]: on "[ab]" it matches with a value, on "[]" it matches with val empty (the
val term is absent and close is there), on "ab" it fails.
rs writes its star on the TERM, before the = (val*^=[a-z]): zero or more, so on "[]" it matches with val empty (RR-9).
RULED (Tony, 2026-11-19): a modifier belongs to the term, written before the =. A * or ? written AFTER a set
(val^=[a-z]*, val^=[a-z]?) refuses by name where the rule is defined, naming the term-first cure (RR-14, RR-15: their
defines are the DEF- lines below, added to that row's input only, because a define-time refusal would land in every
row). A + after a set is the set's own run, one or more, and stays legal (RR-16). Before the ruling rs was written
val^=[a-z]* and RR-9 read "set-star empty false": the suffix star was never read.

MEASURED 2026-11-19, the same on both grammars:
  RR-1, RR-2  if rule(x) and if t = rule(x): value true, EMPTY TRUE, fail false.
  RR-3        if t := rule(x) read true even on a failure (it tested the holder t, always there). RULED (Tony,
              2026-11-19): a := as an if / while / do condition REFUSES by name with the cure, "write t := ...; then
              if *t", on both grammars, body and top level. RR-3, RR-12 (while), RR-13 (do), TP-1 and TP-2 (top level).
  RR-4        while t = rule(x) on the same field: a match loops (capped at 3 by a break), a failure runs no pass.
  RR-5        after if t = rule(x), *t.val REFUSES ("the left of this dot holds nothing"): = made t the result, not a holder.
  RR-6        t := rule(x); if *t; then *t.val reads ab (respelled from if t := rule(x) when that began to refuse).
  RR-10       after if t = rule(x), the unstarred t.val reads ab.
  RR-11       t := rule(x); then if *t: value true, empty true, fail false.
  SO TWO SPELLINGS ARE CONSISTENT: with =, no stars (if t = rule(x); ... t.val); with :=, stars throughout
  (t := rule(x); if *t; ... *t.val). Mixing them is the trap (RR-3, RR-5).
  RR-7        after an empty match close reads ] (it matched); the absent val reads as a tag echo, no refusal.
  RR-8        after a failure, reading a piece refuses by name ("the left of this dot holds nothing").
  RR-9        rs, the star on the term: value true, empty true.

RR-17 TO RR-24 back docs/kantPatterns.md's "calling an action vs calling a rule" and its empty-cell example, pinned as read:
  RR-17  cell isRule val*^=[^,] comma^=",": "ab," and "," both match; after "," the comma piece reads ",".
  RR-18  cells isRule cell+ over ",,": matches, two pieces, and ENDS (an empty piece inside a repeat does not loop).
  RR-19  a rule's code reads its label as *actionLabel (its pieces by name) and hands it back with return *actionLabel.
  RR-20  a rule called with no argument: REFUSES BY NAME on the new road; on the old road it reads false in silence.
  RR-21  a rule called with an argument that has no text: the same split.
  RR-22  an action takes anything as its argument (a count, a string, a group) and hands back what return gives.
  RR-23  an action returning its argument: 1 reads true on both; 0 reads FALSE ON THE OLD ROAD AND TRUE ON THE NEW.
  RR-24  an action with no return reads TRUE in an if, on both roads.

